r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(0)$ and ${r}^{″}(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =2k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-132130/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132130"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-35234"] :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\pi $ for the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t},\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-1}\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{cos}^{2}⁡t,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{sin}^{2}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\pi ,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0+1,{e}^{-1}+0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi )$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{e},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1/e & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1/e\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{e}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{e})}=e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-35114/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "ae1f7d0f-ef9d-56c7-b8ac-41f49fe36560" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-35114/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "682a9c74-e02d-56b0-ab53-052222cefc3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-35114/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" :answer/id #uuid "fc760af9-2de1-5910-8c48-81c477723569" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-35114/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "a94e181c-ca3b-59f1-be43-075fd2a4c7f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-35114/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "d87d4601-f5f1-5b7d-b85d-69971d250dbd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-35114/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e758c140-5a89-5d34-92b7-14516efe1051" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-35114/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-35114/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" "q-35114/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-35114/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" "q-35114/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-35114/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-35114" :question/id #uuid "df705f9d-a2f0-57c1-9293-5cf4d9e544fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-35114" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,\\,-2,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=\\frac{{2}^{3}}{\\,2\\,}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-35114/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-35114"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3338"] :question/problem "Consider a curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $\\mathbf r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s,$ we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(s)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $\\mathbf T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))^{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|} = \\dfrac{1}{\\:\\dfrac{1}{3}\\:} = 3\\,.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3339"] :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at the point $\\mathbf{r}(0),$ if the curve and the derivative $\\mathbf{T}'(t)$ of the corresponding unit tangent vector are given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t, \\:\\dfrac{ t^2}{2}\\right\\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf T'(t)= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{(t^2+1)^3}}\\left\\langle -t, \\:  1\\right\\rangle \\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ and the derivative $\\mathbf T'(t)$ of the unit tangent, we will use the following formula to compute the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\mathbf \\kappa(t)} = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|}{\\left\\| \\mathbf T'(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ $\\mathbf r'(0),$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨1,t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate $\\mathbf T'(0)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{(1+{0}^{2})^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(1+{0}^{2})^{3}}}⟩=⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & = \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩+1\\cdot ⟨0,1⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨0,1⟩ & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/assets/cc9e1eaf1dc8655565a94b7ef0146dc2bb458cc7376828b19352acc01ddbeaad.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3340"] :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=0$ for the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle e^{ t}, \\: e^{- t} \\right\\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $\\mathbf r(t),$ we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(t)} = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}{\\left\\|\\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t),$ $\\mathbf r'(0),$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate $\\mathbf r''(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-(-{e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross product by considering $\\mathbf r'(0)$ and $\\mathbf r''(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\,-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\,-1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}=\\frac{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{3}}{\\,2\\,}=\\sqrt{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
